\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Unless stated otherwise, results are for Bernoulli assignment and $\sigma=0.5$ on the synthetic graphs of Section~\ref{sec:design}; intervals are Monte Carlo intervals with graph draws as the unit of replication. All outcomes are simulated.

\subsection{Variance follows leverage; bias does not}
\label{sec:varbias}

\paragraph{Variance.}
The sampling variance of the regression estimator follows $\sigma^2L/n$. Where the specification is correct (the plain SBM at $\sigma=0.5$, the no-spillover column and mechanism (a) at $s=\tfrac12$; the 42 cells of the $n$, $d$ and $p$ slices), log SD falls with $n$ with slope $-0.50$, rises with $1+d$ with slope $+0.55$ [0.547, 0.556] and falls with $p(1-p)$ with slope $-0.50$ (predicted: $\pm\tfrac12$; Figure~\ref{fig:var}, Appendix~\ref{app:extra}). The $d$ slope is therefore not $\tfrac12$: the realised leverage scales as $L\propto n^{-0.02}(1+d)^{1.09}[p(1-p)]^{-0.99}$ over 210 graph draws, so its square root has exponent $0.55$ in $1+d$. The slope rests on three degrees, and the intervals treat the 42 cells as independent although they share graphs and draws. The SD is smallest at $p=\tfrac12$ in all seven mechanism--degree panels that have a $p$ grid (Figure~\ref{fig:bias}, lower row, and Appendix~\ref{app:extra}).

